items.body:
## Estado reconciliado — Gaudí, 2026-09-07

Parent: #2185  
Coordinates with: #2186, #2187, #2189, #2193, #2879, #2164, #2660 and current domain owners  
Current review baseline: `main@9b6ba4d66631f6ae18dc6503944c68a9e7a2a3bc` / release `0.15.0`  
Review revision: `CRYPTO1.4-RECONCILIATION-R1`  
Administrative state proposed: **ACTIVE P1 — partially delivered; refreshed inventory and complete swarm review required before the next cut**

Claim: https://github.com/dunay2/dvt/issues/2191#issuecomment-5571052231

This issue remains the migration/deletion owner for shared cryptographic primitives. It is not the owner of every domain identity that consumes those primitives.

## Governing rule

```text
domain owns:
  preimage + framing + prefix + truncation + version + compatibility meaning

@dvt/crypto owns:
  SHA-256 + MD5 + JCS + UTF-8/hex + secure random + UUID mechanics
```

A hash or UUID call is not migrated by name alone. Each current call site must first be classified by behavior, persistence, public format, determinism, streaming needs and runtime constraints. Once classified as an active shared-primitive consumer, it imports `@dvt/crypto` directly and its local primitive path is deleted in the same bounded cut.

## Current source truth

### Delivered and not to be reopened

1. The physical/public package `packages/@dvt/crypto` exists and exports the approved primitive surface:
   - `sha256Hex`, `sha256HexUtf8`, `createSha256Hasher`;
   - `md5Hex`, `md5HexUtf8`;
   - `jcsCanonicalize`, `utf8Bytes`, `base64Bytes`;
   - `secureRandomBytes`, `randomUuidV4`, `randomUuidV7`.
2. Current repository search finds no active production import from the former `@dvt/canonical` package path. Historical documents and comments remain historical evidence.
3. The first contracts/planner/verifier/engine SHA/JCS facade cut has executable recurrence protection in the architecture guard. It must remain retired.
4. PR #2897 delivered the bounded Run Execution Context slice:
   - `RunExecutionContextRef.sha256` is strict `Sha256HexString`;
   - `RunExecutionContext.planSha256` is strict `Sha256HexString`;
   - the writer, reader and artifact-path hashing use `@dvt/crypto`;
   - exact `JSON.stringify(context)` bytes, digests and content-addressed locators remain unchanged;
   - malformed digest shape rejects at the contract boundary while byte verification remains defense in depth.
5. Existing migrated domain code, for example engine idempotency hashes, already imports `sha256HexUtf8` while retaining its preimage and identity policy locally.

#2897 is a completed contribution to this issue, not evidence that the global migration is complete.

### Remaining current surface

A commit-bound code search on the reviewed baseline reports:

```text
createHash(   -> 69 TypeScript matches
randomUUID(  -> 21 TypeScript matches
```

Those counts include production, tests and prototypes and are therefore a discovery inventory, not a migration count. Concrete current production examples include:

- `apps/api/src/entrypoints/http/recoverRunIdentity.ts` — SHA-256 over a NUL-framed tenant/source-run/idempotency preimage, truncated to 40 hex characters and prefixed `run_recovery_`;
- `packages/@dvt/delivery/src/outboxShardAssignment.ts` — MD5 over tenant-affine shard input, first 16 hex characters, signed-int64 conversion and modulo;
- `packages/@dvt/artifacts/src/runtime/readVerifiedArtifactBytes.ts` — exact-byte artifact SHA-256 verification;
- DBT bundle/archive/upload/content-revision paths — one-shot and streaming content identity;
- Postgres/workspace/audit/application identity and integrity paths;
- engine/API/Web UUID allocation paths with different meanings, including event IDs, public run-related IDs, temporary file names and browser idempotency keys;
- tests, scripts and `infra/prototypes/**` that require an explicit retain/migrate/freeze/delete disposition rather than implicit exemption.

These examples do not prove that every hit needs the same change. They prove that the final inventory and deletion/exception proof are still incomplete.

## Problem classification

The architectural problem is not the existence of domain-specific hashes. It is multiple direct access paths to the same low-level mechanics:

```text
domain policy -> node:crypto / WebCrypto / uuid mechanics
```

instead of:

```text
domain policy -> @dvt/crypto primitive
```

Consequences:

- primitive implementation and runtime behavior can drift;
- consumer packages can depend transitively rather than declare the real dependency;
- byte compatibility becomes harder to prove because mechanics and preimage policy are mixed;
- broad scanners cannot distinguish approved protocol/runtime exceptions from accidental duplication;
- old migration manifests become misleading as files are delivered, renamed or deleted.

## Required refreshed inventory

#2186 or an Ada-owned refreshed comment on this issue must produce a machine-reproducible inventory for the exact implementation head. Every hit receives one disposition:

| Disposition | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `MIGRATE_EXACT_BYTES` | active one-shot byte digest; move mechanics, preserve bytes |
| `MIGRATE_STREAMING` | active bounded-memory digest; use `createSha256Hasher()` |
| `MIGRATE_TEXT_PREIMAGE` | active UTF-8 identity; preserve exact framing/prefix/truncation |
| `MIGRATE_UUID_OR_RANDOM` | active identity/entropy allocation; preserve version, variant, prefix and failure semantics |
| `APPROVED_RUNTIME_EXCEPTION` | protocol/deterministic-runtime constraint with exact file, symbol and reason |
| `TEST_ONLY` | test fixture/helper; centralize or retain only when behavior proof requires it |
| `PROTOTYPE_FREEZE_OR_DELETE` | inactive prototype; do not harden it as product code |
| `ALREADY_DELIVERED` | current source already imports the authority and former path is absent |
| `DEAD_DELETE` | no active responsibility or consumer |

Required inventory fields:

```text
file + symbol
production/config/test/prototype reachability
identity/integrity meaning
exact preimage or bytes
algorithm + encoding
streaming/memory constraint
public/persisted compatibility obligation
current tests/golden vectors
consumer package dependency
bounded implementation owner
final disposition
```

Names alone are not evidence. #2879 owns the exhaustive follow-up classification of digest-shaped public contract fields after excluding fields already corrected here.

## Selected delivery strategy

Use bounded semantic cuts. Do not repeat the obsolete all-at-once file manifest against current source.

### Cut A — exact-byte artifact and DBT integrity

Candidate responsibility:

- generic artifact read/verify;
- DBT bundles, tar archives, uploads, source snapshots and content revisions;
- workspace/file integrity where exact bytes are the contract.

Solution:

- one-shot bytes use `sha256Hex(bytes)`;
- bounded streams use `createSha256Hasher()`;
- media type, size, tenant path and immutable locator rules stay with artifacts/DBT owners;
- no JCS substitution for exact stored bytes;
- no full-buffer rewrite of an existing streaming path.

Coordinate with #2660 so dead or superseded artifact surfaces are deleted rather than migrated for completeness.

### Cut B — domain text identities and recovery

Candidate responsibility:

- recovery run identity;
- persisted workspace/edit/governance/analysis identities whose preimage is text or canonical JSON;
- API and state identities still calling platform SHA directly.

Solution:

- retain each named/versioned preimage in its domain;
- use `sha256HexUtf8` only where the existing contract hashes exactly one UTF-8 string;
- use byte/incremental primitives where concatenation would alter framing;
- preserve prefixes, NUL/length framing, case, truncation and normalization exactly;
- any changed output requires an explicit identity-version migration, not regenerated snapshots.

For `deriveRecoveryRunId`, the expected equivalent result remains:

```text
SHA256(tenantId + NUL + sourceRunId + NUL + idempotencyKey)
 -> first 40 lowercase hex
 -> prefix run_recovery_
```

### Cut C — Delivery MD5 compatibility

Candidate responsibility:

- `packages/@dvt/delivery/src/outboxShardAssignment.ts` only, plus direct consumers/tests.

Solution:

- replace only MD5 mechanics with `md5HexUtf8`;
- retain tenant-affine input framing;
- retain first-16-hex truncation, signed-int64 conversion and modulo;
- prove all existing shard vectors and distribution invariants are byte-for-byte unchanged.

MD5 remains a compatibility primitive for shard assignment, not a security claim.

### Cut D — UUID and secure entropy

Candidate responsibility:

- engine/API/Web public/domain IDs;
- browser idempotency keys;
- temporary publication/file names;
- test factories.

Solution after classification:

- UUIDv4 consumers use `randomUuidV4()` when current format/version is v4;
- UUIDv7 consumers use `randomUuidV7()` while retaining platform prefixes and timestamp policy;
- raw entropy uses `secureRandomBytes()` and fails closed;
- Temporal workflow deterministic randomness remains an explicit narrow exception where applicable;
- temporary-file uniqueness is classified separately from public identity but still cannot justify a duplicate convenience wrapper;
- no `Date.now()`/`Math.random()` fallback.

### Cut E — scripts, tools, tests and prototypes

- migrate active release/governance/runtime tooling through the same package when it is a real consumer;
- centralize primitive-only vectors under `@dvt/crypto`;
- retain domain tests with their domain owners;
- delete/freeze inactive prototypes instead of adding production dependencies;
- every exception is file/symbol-specific with owner and reason;
- no broad directory allowlist.

### Cut F — recurrence and closeout

Owned by #2193 after migration cuts:

- repository guard over production, workers, scripts, tools and tests;
- direct dependency proof for every active consumer package;
- zero former package/facade/alias paths;
- domain compatibility and real-byte tamper proof;
- before/after counts and explicit exceptions;
- no second migration phase hidden inside the guard issue.

## Hard-cut rules

For every implementation cut:

```text
freeze domain/public vectors
 -> delete or neutralize the old primitive path first in the branch
 -> capture compiler/scanner/test discovery
 -> import @dvt/crypto directly in every surfaced consumer
 -> preserve or explicitly version domain identity
 -> delete old source/export/dependency/primitive-only test
 -> prove zero obsolete reachability
 -> finish green
```

Broken intermediate commits may exist in the branch as discovery evidence; broken code is never merged. When repository policy or owner direction permits equivalent recorded deletion-first RED evidence without a broken commit, document that decision as #2897 did. No wrapper, forwarding export, alias, dual implementation, feature flag or insecure fallback survives the final head.

## Package and dependency rule

Every active consumer package declares a direct `@dvt/crypto` dependency. Domains import primitives directly; contracts, engine, artifacts, planner and verifier do not re-export them for convenience.

Do not introduce a generic `hash(kind, value)` API. Explicit primitive names keep algorithm and representation visible.

## Compatibility matrix per consumer

Every public/persisted/domain output chooses exactly one result:

- **byte-equivalent:** same version and exact output;
- **intentionally changed:** new domain identity version, migration/read policy and removal condition;
- **confirmed unused internal behavior:** delete with reachability/build evidence;
- **approved exception:** no migration, with exact runtime/protocol reason and recurrence-guard entry.

Do not update golden values until an unexplained output change is resolved.

## Required tests and evidence

Per bounded cut:

- exact old/new vectors for empty, ASCII, Unicode and domain-specific edge cases;
- exact public prefixes/length/version/variant where applicable;
- one-shot vs streaming SHA-256 equality;
- bounded-memory proof for streaming files/bundles;
- tampered bytes and mismatched metadata reject at the owned reader/admission boundary;
- direct package dependency and import proof;
- zero old source/export/path occurrences;
- affected builds/typechecks/domain tests/service-backed tests;
- ARC evidence/risk updates for changes under `@dvt/contracts` or other governed surfaces;
- final `pnpm verify:prepush`.

Do not mock the verifier being tested. Alter real bytes or metadata.

## Help requested before the next implementation cut

- **Eisenhower:** reconcile #2185/#2186/#2187/#2189/#2191/#2193 status against current delivery, sequence the remaining semantic cuts with #2164/#2660 and obtain current Planning DB ownership/rail evidence.
- **Ada:** produce the exact-head consumer/dependency/reachability inventory and propose the smallest non-overlapping TDD cuts.
- **Dijkstra:** validate byte-equivalence proofs, framing/truncation mathematics, streaming equivalence and UUID version/variant invariants.
- **Atila:** identify every former implementation, facade, export, alias, dependency and primitive-only test to delete; reject wrappers and broad allowlists.
- **Velázquez:** classify Web/public-ID and visible failure impacts; confirm unchanged user-visible formats or emit a motivated objection.
- **Deming:** define and later execute an independent scanner, compatibility, tamper, package-dependency and final-diff verification. The implementer cannot self-certify by changing role name.
- **Executor with Planning DB access:** run `pnpm planning:db:query architecture-designs --limit 100` and post only relevant crypto/identity/integrity component and rail identities. Do not import or rebuild the Planning DB.

## Consensus gate

Under #3003, `CRYPTO1.4-RECONCILIATION-R1` is not accepted until Eisenhower, Gaudí, Dijkstra, Ada, Atila, Velázquez and Deming respond to this exact revision/SHA with `APORTE`, `OBJECIÓN` or motivated `FUERA_DE_COMPETENCIA`, and all competent objections/conditions are resolved. Silence or timeout is `AUSENTE/TIMEOUT`, never consent. Dunay retains product priority and final acceptance.

## Definition of Ready for the next cut

- [x] current package and completed Run Execution Context slice verified;
- [x] remaining direct-call surface demonstrated on current `main`;
- [x] domain-policy vs primitive-authority boundary fixed;
- [x] semantic cut strategy proposed;
- [ ] exact implementation-head inventory and dispositions complete;
- [ ] open PR/issue ownership overlap reconciled per file family;
- [ ] live Planning DB ownership/rail evidence posted;
- [ ] complete seven-role review of `CRYPTO1.4-RECONCILIATION-R1` recorded;
- [ ] one bounded cut selected with frozen compatibility vectors and direct dependency list.

## Definition of Done

- [ ] every active shared primitive consumer imports `@dvt/crypto` directly;
- [ ] every active consumer package declares the dependency;
- [ ] direct SHA-256/MD5/JCS/identity-random mechanics outside crypto are zero except explicit narrow exceptions;
- [ ] former `@dvt/canonical` package/path and primitive forwarding exports are absent from active code/config;
- [ ] no local primitive facade, wrapper, insecure fallback or dual implementation remains;
- [ ] domain preimages, versions, prefixes, truncations, shard outputs and public formats are preserved or explicitly versioned;
- [ ] streaming consumers remain bounded-memory;
- [ ] primitive-only tests are centralized and domain behavior tests remain with their owners;
- [ ] #2879 has an explicit disposition for remaining digest-shaped public fields;
- [ ] #2193 recurrence/compatibility proof and closeout metrics pass;
- [ ] current docs and Planning DB name only surviving authorities;
- [ ] Deming posts independent final evidence on the delivered exact head;
- [ ] all required package/domain/service-backed/architecture/docs and pre-push checks are green.

## Non-goals

- moving domain preimages or identity policy into crypto;
- replacing named algorithms with a generic hash service;
- changing algorithms or outputs under a cleanup label;
- treating every `hash`, `fingerprint` or UUID-shaped field as equivalent;
- reworking JOSE/protocol libraries or Temporal deterministic workflow behavior;
- hardening dead compatibility or prototype code instead of deleting/freezing it;
- reopening the completed #2897 slice;
- adding another inventory framework, registry, facade or compatibility bridge.

No consensus, next implementation cut or epic closure is claimed while the refreshed inventory, role responses and Planning DB evidence are absent.

— **Gaudí, Principal / Staff Software Architect — Dancing Gang**

Parent: #2185

Depends on: #2189, #2191

## Goal

Close the epic with repository-level proof that the single `@dvt/crypto` authority preserves current behavior and that duplicate implementations cannot return.

This issue adds proof and guards only. It must not become another migration phase.

## Required proof

### Primitive parity

Run the central `packages/@dvt/crypto/test/**` corpus in every runtime currently supported by the package design. Do not add hypothetical runtimes only for this issue.

Prove:

- SHA-256 one-shot and streaming equality;
- standard SHA-256 and MD5 vectors;
- RFC 8785/JCS Unicode and ordering behavior;
- UUIDv4/v7 version and variant behavior;
- secure-random fail-closed behavior;
- identical applicable output across supported runtimes.

### Domain compatibility

Replay the existing domain vectors and prove unchanged behavior for at least:

- planner input identity and `planId`;
- exact executable-plan/PlanRef digest;
- artifact and compiled-code references;
- event/start/recovery idempotency and public ID formats;
- DBT project/content/archive/analysis revisions;
- governance fingerprints;
- workspace/warehouse identities owned by #2171, #2173 and #2174;
- Delivery outbox shard assignment;
- web idempotency prefixes and API `run_` identity shape.

Where a migration was explicitly approved, prove both the bounded old reader and new writer path and its removal condition.

### Tamper/integrity

For exact-byte boundaries, mutate real bytes/metadata and prove rejection at the owned reader/admission boundary. Do not mock the verifier being tested.

## Recurrence guard

Fail CI when active source or tests outside `packages/@dvt/crypto/**` introduce:

- `createHash('sha256')` or `createHash('md5')`;
- `subtle.digest('SHA-256', ...)`;
- handwritten SHA/MD5 logic;
- local JCS/canonical serializers or direct `json-canonicalize` ownership;
- direct identity allocation through `randomBytes`, `randomUUID`, `getRandomValues` or manual UUID formatting;
- `Date.now()`/`Math.random()` identity fallbacks;
- crypto primitive re-exports from another DVT package;
- primitive-only test suites outside the central package;
- consumer packages lacking a direct `@dvt/crypto` dependency;
- restoration of `packages/@dvt/canonical` or current references to it.

Narrow exclusions may cover only:

- specialized `jose` protocol behavior;
- Temporal deterministic workflow randomness;
- clearly non-security, non-identity randomness.

Exclusions are explicit files/symbols with a reason, never broad directory wildcards.

## Closeout report

Post to #2185:

- before/after primitive implementation count;
- before/after direct platform-call count;
- deleted source files;
- deleted primitive-only tests;
- retained domain suites;
- runtime/vector results;
- domain compatibility results;
- tamper cases and rejection result;
- any remaining narrow exclusion.

## Definition of Ready

- [ ] #2189 central package and primitive corpus are green.
- [ ] #2191 consumer migration and deletion work is complete.
- [ ] No transition wrapper or old package path remains intentionally.
- [ ] Supported runtime list is fixed by #2187.
- [ ] Domain golden values and migration cases are available.

## Definition of Done

- [ ] All central primitive/runtime tests pass.
- [ ] All required domain compatibility vectors pass.
- [ ] Exact-byte tamper cases are rejected.
- [ ] Source guards cover product, workers, scripts, tools and tests.
- [ ] Direct SHA-256/MD5/JCS/identity-random implementation calls outside crypto are zero, except narrow approved exclusions.
- [ ] Primitive re-export wrappers are zero.
- [ ] Primitive-only suites outside crypto are zero.
- [ ] Old `packages/@dvt/canonical` paths/references are zero.
- [ ] Existing product/domain suites remain green.
- [ ] Closeout metrics are posted to #2185.
- [ ] The epic can close without a manual follow-up audit.

## Not done when

- only `sha256('abc')` is tested;
- hashes are snapshot-updated without compatibility explanation;
- guards cover product code but omit scripts/tests;
- broad allowlists hide directories;
- transition wrappers remain;
- mutation tests mock rather than alter real bytes.

Parent epic: #2152

## Phase

Gate A — repository-grounded claim baseline. This task is read-only with respect to product behavior.

## Current verified starting point

The repository already contains substantial planning/runtime functionality. The research programme must not describe delivered components as gaps or describe conceptual targets as implemented facts.

Known anchors to verify against current `main` include:

- `packages/@dvt/contracts/src/contracts/planner/ExecutionPlan.v1.ts`;
- `packages/@dvt/contracts/src/contracts/planner/PlanExecutionDecision.v1.ts`;
- `packages/@dvt/planner/src/domain/PlanExecutionDecisionProjector.ts`;
- `@dvt/plan-interpreter`, immutable `PlanRef`, state-store and run-event tests;
- `@dvt/adapter-temporal` and its integration suites;
- `docs/architecture/system-delivery-status.md` and current Planning DB architecture/mechanization reads;
- delivered Preview decision work in #174/#2128 and the closed executable vertical #2106.

## Objective

Produce one immutable, commit-bound research baseline that answers, for every candidate DVT claim:

```text
what exactly exists?
where is it implemented?
which executable evidence proves it?
what is partial or absent?
which statement is only a hypothesis?
```

The baseline is historical study evidence for a specific commit. It is not a new current-status authority and must not duplicate the generated documentation work under #2143.

## Value

| Dimension | Value | Measure |
|---|---|---|
| Scientific | prevents a thesis from claiming existing architecture or intended work as novelty | 100% of research claims classified and source-linked |
| Product | prevents duplicate implementation issues | zero new implementation issue justified only by an already-delivered capability |
| Delivery | gives every later R1 task an exact starting point | every later issue can cite an exact contract/code/test baseline |
| Cost | stops unsupported work before code is written | all absent-value or duplicate paths explicitly marked `stop`/`reuse` |

## Definition of Ready

In addition to the epic DoR:

- [ ] the exact `main` commit under study is fixed;
- [ ] active PRs/issues touching planner decisions, plan contracts, runtime providers or state evidence are listed;
- [ ] the initial claim catalogue is agreed and limited to this epic:
  - deterministic canonical planning;
  - safe partial execution;
  - machine-verifiable decision evidence;
  - immutable/replayable execution evidence;
  - provider-neutral runtime semantics;
  - measurable cost/performance/user value;
- [ ] existing repository/Planning DB queries and package verification commands are available;
- [ ] the research baseline location is selected through the existing documentation lifecycle, as a commit-bound study artifact rather than a maintained current-status page.

## Concrete work

1. Resolve the exact current code, contract, test and documentation anchors for every claim.
2. Run the existing relevant validation commands; do not create a new validation framework.
3. For each claim, record:
   - precise statement;
   - status: `implemented`, `partially implemented`, `absent`, `not testable`, or `hypothesis`;
   - owning component/contract;
   - exact code and test paths;
   - observed command/result;
   - known limitations and counterexamples;
   - existing issue/PR ownership when work is already open.
4. Demonstrate explicitly that current `RUN/SKIP/PARTIAL` projection is selection-closure explainability, not yet proof of semantic skip safety, unless current `main` has changed that fact.
5. Verify the active provider posture and the closure reason of legacy Conductor issue #69.
6. Search for effect semantics, skip-safety evidence and provider-conformance ownership; classify absence only after exact repository search and source inspection.
7. Produce a concise gap list containing only gaps that survive the audit.
8. Link the baseline from #2152 and R1.2; do not convert it into another product status dashboard.

## Required evidence and checks

- exact path and symbol resolution for every positive implementation claim;
- at least one executable test/command for every claim marked `implemented` where such evidence should exist;
- an explicit counterexample or missing-contract demonstration for every claim marked `absent`/`partial`;
- link validation and current documentation checks for the bounded research artifact;
- repository and Planning DB reads must agree or the conflict is reported, not reconciled by inference;
- review of open issues proves that no R1 gap already has an active owner.

## Metrics and stop condition

- claim coverage: **100%** of the initial catalogue;
- unsupported positive claims: **0**;
- claims based only on prose/issue text when code evidence is expected: **0**;
- unresolved authority conflicts: **0** before closure;
- duplicate implementation candidates found: all are removed from the later R1 scope.

If safe partial execution or provider conformance is already fully implemented and evidenced on current `main`, this task must narrow or cancel the corresponding later work rather than preserve the original roadmap.

## Definition of Done

- [ ] one commit-bound AS-IS baseline exists at the approved canonical research/evidence location;
- [ ] every claim has status, owner, exact sources, tests/commands and limitations;
- [ ] the current decision projector and provider posture are described without extrapolation;
- [ ] surviving gaps are reproducible and linked to later R1 tasks;
- [ ] duplicated or obsolete proposed work is explicitly removed from #2152;
- [ ] no product code, new registry, generated current-status page or Planning DB task state was introduced;
- [ ] relevant docs/link/governance checks pass;
- [ ] a reviewer can independently reproduce the classification from the cited commit.

## Explicitly insufficient outcomes

- summarizing conceptual DVT documents without reading current code;
- copying `system-delivery-status.md` into another file;
- treating file existence as proof of semantics;
- marking a claim implemented because an issue was closed;
- listing gaps without counterexamples or exact missing seams;
- creating a manually maintained architecture or task matrix;
- proposing implementation before this baseline is reviewed.

## Dependencies and closure

No R1 implementation task is allowed to enter Ready before this issue is complete. R1.2 may begin literature collection in parallel, but it cannot delimit novelty until this AS-IS baseline is fixed.

Parent: #3289
Coordinates with: #3265, #3268, #3269, #3270, #2195, #2919, #2937.
Blocks design freeze/migration under #3294-#3296 until the interaction contract below is accepted.

## Outcome

Resolve the remaining contradictions between the UX specifications, the current Figma prototype and earlier RELTREE1 interaction rules before treating the prototype as migration-ready.

This task freezes product behavior. It does not create a new authoring model, store, persistence rail or execution path.

## Decisions superseded / clarified

### Contextual insertion slots

UXFIG1 supersedes only the **presentation prohibition** in #3265/#3268 that said there must be no generic plus-sign authoring affordance.

New rule:

- `+` is a contextual insertion affordance only;
- hidden in resting state;
- appears on hover, keyboard focus or compatible drag;
- invokes the existing admitted-operation chooser/command path;
- never represents semantic truth, persistence or an operation by itself;
- no always-visible chain of plus signs.

The underlying RELTREE1 rule remains: operation availability and mutation authority come from the existing admitted capability/authoring rails.

### Model double-click

Freeze:

- double-click on a Model card -> open **Editor semántico**;
- data inspection remains in the existing bottom drawer; no SQL/Data action strip in the Model editor;
- selecting the Model remains selection/focus only;
- opening data must not compete with the double-click authoring gesture.

### Selection vs expansion

Freeze:

- first click/focus selects an operation;
- selection does not automatically expand it;
- explicit `Expandir` / `Focus` opens editable detail;
- zoom changes only scale; detail expansion is explicitly controlled and remains unchanged;
- moving a visual card/layout never means reordering semantic operations.

## Model workspace navigation (approved 2026-09-26)

One workspace navigation row, with the actual model name:

```text
Canvas | <Model name>
```

Model Data, operation Data, `Problemas` and `Registro` remain in the existing bottom operational drawer. SQL is one read-only projection of the applied revision; its request gesture and display location are deliberately NOT decided in this slice. Do not add a SQL tab, SQL button, or second data entry point to the Model editor. No second Model/SQL/Data strip.

Do not ship a separate `Visual` view until it has a non-overlapping product purpose.

`Datos` and data samples opened from nodes must reuse the same preview/data-result infrastructure and preserve which result/context is being inspected.

## Apply / Save / Preview contract

The current source already separates local editor draft, authoring aggregate, durable draft save and Preview/Run. Preserve that split.

### Local edit draft

- edits inside JOIN/Window/etc. may remain local/incomplete;
- local draft does not affect SQL, Datos or the Model until Apply;
- Cancel/Escape discards the local draft with zero semantic write.

### Apply

`Apply` means:

```text
valid local editor draft
 -> existing Model/Canvas authoring command
 -> current Model working draft / WorkspaceGraphAuthoringDraft working state
```

It does **not** publish data and does **not** execute the Model.

After Apply:

- Editor semántico and SQL use the newly applied working revision;
- any Preview/Data result based on the prior revision becomes stale;
- stale results remain inspectable but are clearly marked and never presented as current.

### Save

`Save` means durable persistence of the current authoring draft through the existing save/CAS/autosave rail.

Do not create another persistence command for the Editor semántico.

The product may rely on current autosave; in that case the visible contract is status (`Guardando`, `Guardado`, conflict/failure) rather than a second semantic Save button. For the golden path, “guardar” means obtaining the successful durable-save receipt before reload.

### Preview / Datos

`Vista previa` executes/queries the **current applied Model revision** without publishing the destination.

- Preview never reads incomplete local editor state.
- It may run against the current applied working draft according to the surviving preview rail; durable publication is not required.
- Preview/Data output is tagged to the exact Model/draft revision/signature used.
- Apply after Preview invalidates freshness immediately.
- Data remains visible with an `Obsoleto` state until preview is rerun.
- Execute/Publish readiness remains separate from semantic validity and Preview freshness.

### SQL

SQL is a projection of the current **applied** Model revision.

- incomplete local edits are not reflected;
- Apply updates SQL projection;
- SQL is not an independent mutation authority in this scope.

## Navigation with pending changes

- leaving Focus with an incomplete/unapplied local edit must not silently lose it;
- prompt/affordance must offer `Aplicar`, `Descartar` or `Permanecer` where navigation would destroy the local draft;
- ordinary in-app navigation after Apply keeps the working authoring session;
- reload/browser close while durable save is pending follows the existing draft lifecycle/recovery boundary and must never claim saved state prematurely.

## Undo contract

Separate two concepts:

1. local editor draft -> Cancel/Escape; no semantic Undo entry is created before Apply;
2. applied semantic edit -> use existing semantic authoring history/undo only if a real current authority exists.

If current source has no semantic undo owner, the first vertical slice must not invent one merely for UX parity. `Edit > Undo` for semantic edits remains gated until source-first proof identifies the surviving command-history owner.

Visual layout movement/repositioning is not semantic undo/reorder.

## N-input composition contract

Adding input 3+ must answer visually:

- which existing result/subtree receives the new input;
- which operation incorporates it;
- orientation/order when asymmetric;
- required predicate/schema configuration;
- whether the new input is participating or merely connected/pending.

For accumulated JOIN presentation, compact grouping is allowed only when the user can reveal the exact steps/types/conditions that matter.

Dragging a card to improve layout is a presentation action. Reordering semantic operands/operations requires an explicit semantic command/gesture.

## Capability tiers

Every designed feature is classified as one of:

### DELIVERABLE NOW

- current Source/Model topology;
- current admitted INNER JOIN authoring;
- repeatable N-input JOIN path already owned by RELTREE1;
- multiple admitted JOIN predicates/groups through the existing predicate editor;
- Editor semántico / SQL / Datos views;
- Preview without publication;
- durable save/reload through existing draft persistence;
- contextual Focus/Expand;
- aliases/references where current identity model supports them.

### DESIGN NOW, DELIVERY GATED BY CURRENT AUTHORITY

- CTE creation/reuse;
- broader Window functions/frames beyond admitted support;
- operations beyond the admitted catalogue;
- semantic Undo if no existing command-history authority is proven.

### OUT OF SCOPE

- new relational IR/AST/store;
- automatic optimizer/reordering;
- implicit join discovery;
- publishing runtime redesign;
- preserving obsolete editor surfaces for compatibility.

## First vertical slice acceptance

The first production delivery is not complete until one browser story proves:

```text
open Model
 -> Editor semántico
 -> use three Sources
 -> create/extend JOIN
 -> edit multiple conditions
 -> Apply
 -> verify canonical projection independently (SQL presentation deferred)
 -> Preview / inspect Datos without publication
 -> observe exact current result context
 -> persist draft successfully
 -> reload
 -> same Model semantics and inputs
 -> return to Canvas with Model/context preserved
```

Also prove:

- changing a condition after Preview marks prior data stale;
- Cancel from incomplete edit performs zero semantic write;
- navigation away with local unapplied edits is explicit;
- moving visual cards does not reorder semantics;
- keyboard path reaches the same authoring actions.

## Visual corrections required before design acceptance

- inspection and editing use the fixed right inspector; no floating editor;
- no permanently visible `+` chain;
- remove `Visual` and top-level `Problemas` view from the Model strip;
- compact headers;
- minimap optional;
- no permanent advice/tips panel;
- `Ajustar vista` frames the complete semantic composition;
- zoom never changes the drawing structure or expansion state.

## Definition of Done

- [ ] #3289 and #3294 reference this contract as UX authority for migration;
- [ ] contradictions with #3265/#3268 are explicitly scoped to presentation, not semantic authority;
- [ ] Figma screens reflect the frozen decisions;
- [ ] first vertical slice is the migration acceptance path;
- [ ] capability-tier matrix is present in planning/migration docs;
- [ ] no new authoring/persistence/preview authority is introduced.

